{
  "subject": "Basic Mathematics",
  "topic": "Systems of Equations",
  "introduction": "Systems of equations involve finding values that satisfy multiple equations simultaneously. Linear systems use substitution or elimination. Non-linear systems (involving x², xy terms) often require clever algebraic manipulation. In SIMAK, you're often asked for combinations like x+y or x-y rather than individual values.",
  "key_formulas": [
    {
      "name": "Substitution",
      "formula": "\\text{Solve one eq for a variable, substitute into other}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Elimination",
      "formula": "\\text{Multiply equations so one variable cancels when added/subtracted}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Cramer's Rule (2×2)",
      "formula": "x = \\frac{D_x}{D}, \\quad y = \\frac{D_y}{D}, \\quad D = \\begin{vmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{vmatrix}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Three-variable trick",
      "formula": "\\text{Add all three equations, divide by 2 to get x+y+z}"
    }
  ],
  "tips_and_tricks": [
    "For 3-variable systems, try adding all equations to get sum x+y+z quickly.",
    "If asked for x+y, use elimination to eliminate z (not necessarily finding x,y individually).",
    "Non-linear: if you see x+y=s and xy=p, use x-y=±√(s²-4p).",
    "Symmetric systems: try adding and subtracting equations before individual solving.",
    "Matrix determinants: D = ad - bc for 2×2 systems."
  ],
  "worked_examples": [
    {
      "title": "Example 1: Three-variable quick trick",
      "question": "Given: x+y=7, y+z=9, x+z=8. Find x+y+z.",
      "solution": [
        "Step 1: Add all three equations: 2x+2y+2z = 24.",
        "Step 2: Divide by 2: x+y+z = 12.",
        "Step 3: Individual values: z=(x+y+z)-(x+y)=12-7=5, x=12-9=3, y=12-8=4."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Example 2: Non-linear symmetric system",
      "question": "If x+y=5 and x²+y²=13, find xy.",
      "solution": [
        "Step 1: Use identity: (x+y)² = x²+2xy+y².",
        "Step 2: 25 = 13 + 2xy.",
        "Step 3: 2xy = 12 → xy = 6.",
        "Step 4: x and y are roots of t²-5t+6=0: (t-2)(t-3)=0. So {x,y} = {2,3}."
      ]
    }
  ],
